-- A chain keyword in operand position must be on the operand's own line.
-- A keyword on the next line is not absorbed into the operand, because there
-- it is routinely the head of the next declaration.
-- See infix-under-connective.l4 for the operators as later operands.
--
-- This file has no section headers on purpose: with one in the file, the
-- definitions below stop telling apart an operand chain that may continue on
-- the next line from one that may not.

GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      lo IS A NUMBER
      hi IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
x `lies from` lo `up to` hi MEANS x AT LEAST lo AND x AT MOST hi

-- `up to` is a keyword of the operator above and is also the name of the
-- definition that follows the operand `2`, which must still parse as a
-- definition.
qq MEANS 1 PLUS
2
`up to` a MEANS a

-- The same holds for a bare-word keyword.
GIVEN x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
x BOUNDEDBY y MEANS IF x GREATER THAN y THEN y ELSE x

pp MEANS 1 PLUS
2
BOUNDEDBY a MEANS a

#EVAL qq
#EVAL `up to` 7
#EVAL pp
#EVAL BOUNDEDBY 7
#ASSERT qq EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `up to` 7 EQUALS 7
#ASSERT pp EQUALS 3
#ASSERT BOUNDEDBY 7 EQUALS 7
